li.p:
We have
\left[\begin{array}{rrr} 3 \amp 2 \amp 0 \\ -1 \amp 0 \amp -2 \\ 2 \amp 1 \amp 1 \end{array}\right] \sim \left[\begin{array}{rrr} 1 \amp 0 \amp 2 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp -3 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \end{array}\right].
From the reduced row echelon form, we see that x_3 is a free variable and that we have
\begin{alignedat}{2} x_1 \amp {}={} -2x_3 \\ x_2 \amp {}={} 3x_3\text{.} \\ \end{alignedat}
The solution space is then written parametrically as
\xvec=\threevec{x_1}{x_2}{x_3} = x_3\threevec{-2}{3}{1}\text{.}

If we set x_3=1, then we have the solution
\xvec=\threevec{-2}{3}{1}, which says that

  -2\vvec_1+3\vvec_2+\vvec_3 = \zerovec\text{.}
